    title: "The life of a review, and what makes one acceptable",
    body: "A review is not a rubber stamp and not a rewrite: it is a set of specific corrections to the parts of a Blueprint that are wrong. Nothing is pushed at a reviewer. Work is claimed from the reviewer's Queue, which holds the jobs their accreditation, Band and availability allow, with the scope, the window and the fee shown before they claim. Leaving a job in the Queue costs nothing; claiming it starts the clock. The reviewer reviews section by section, correcting what is wrong and saying why (vague disagreement is not a correction); the customer then accepts the corrections or pushes back, and accepted corrections are what the reviewer's standing is built from. Fees are released from Escrow once the review is accepted or the customer's response window closes without a dispute. A disputed review goes to a senior reviewer to adjudicate; their decision settles whether the review counts as accepted, and the fee follows it. Peer Review is the free check for a customer on the free Membership: it carries no money, earns MXP, and is where a new reviewer builds a record. What makes a review acceptable, at every rung: it points at a Section and states what is wrong with it; somebody else could verify the correction without taking your word for it; it is returned inside the window the job carries (late work is declined work with extra steps); it is conflict-free; it is proportionate (a minor Blueprint does not need a major rewrite, and a major flaw is not a footnote); and it is the reviewer's own work (a review generated by a model is not a review). If a reviewer disagrees with a whole Blueprint, they say so in the review with reasons: it has to be argued, not asserted. Leaving work does not count against a reviewer; reviewing outside their competence does. Source: reviewer-process.json, reviewer-payouts.json.",
