// -------- written answers (graded by Claude against the rubric)
items[0].questions.push(open(6,
  "An applicant emails: \"I have fifteen years as a solution architect. Can I skip the Calibration, start at the top rung, and how much will I earn?\" Write the reply, using only what Moksy has published.",
  "Thank you for your interest. Everybody sits the Calibration, a 45 to 60 minute blind review of a document from your own field with planted defects, because a level typed into a form is not evidence. Your experience does count: it sets what passing is worth, meaning the rung and Band you start on, so a long career does not land you at the bottom. The top rung, Principal, is by invitation on a record Moksy can point at, so it is not somewhere to start. After you are accredited, your first three reviews are read by a senior reviewer before they reach the customer, and they are paid in full at the ordinary rate. On earnings, reviews are priced and paid in MOX, and our payouts page gives worked examples of what reaches a reviewer rather than a promise of volume, so I cannot give you a figure. You can apply in about ten minutes by telling us your Track, how long you have worked in it, your current title and a couple of pieces of work.",
  "A good answer: says everybody sits the Calibration and explains why (self-declared level is not evidence); says experience sets the rung and Band they start on and that Principal is by invitation; mentions the first three reviews are read by a senior reviewer and paid in full; does not give an income figure or promise (the page gives worked examples, not a promise of volume); mentions the ~10-minute application and what it asks; is courteous and plain. Penalise promising to skip the Calibration, promising a top rung, or quoting an income.",
  "Applicant questions"));
items[4].questions.push(open(6,
  "A Reviewer-rung reviewer claimed a Section Review and then realises the plan recommends a tool that their employer resells. What should they do, which parts of the code of conduct apply, and what does it cost them?",
  "They should declare the conflict now and decline or stop the review rather than reviewing around it. The code says to declare a conflict before accepting work, not after; a conflict includes any interest in the tools or suppliers the plan recommends, and disclosure is not disqualification while concealment is. Independence applies too: they cannot review the plan on its merits if they have an interest in the recommended tool, and the code says that if you cannot be independent about a piece of work you decline it, and declining costs nothing. Leaving work does not count against them, whereas hiding the conflict would be a breach with consequences ranging from a correction to a level reduction or suspension, reviewed by a senior reviewer or Moksy.",
  "A good answer: tells them to declare the conflict and decline/stop; cites the conflicts-of-interest rule (declare before accepting; supplier/tool interest is a conflict; disclosure is not disqualification, concealment is); cites independence and that declining costs nothing; notes that leaving work does not count against them; mentions the consequences of concealment (correction, level reduction, suspension; reviewed by a senior reviewer or Moksy). Penalise advice to continue quietly or to 'review fairly anyway'.",
  "Conduct"));
items[2].questions.push(open(7,
  "A Contributor's review of a customer's Blueprint says only: \"The architecture is weak and the costs look wrong.\" Explain, in feedback addressed to the reviewer, why this does not meet Moksy's standard, and show what an acceptable version would contain.",
  "Thanks for the review. As written it is not yet a correction. A review points at a Section and states what is wrong with it, and vague disagreement is not a correction. \"Weak\" and \"look wrong\" do not tell the customer which Section is affected, why it is wrong, or what would fix it, and nobody else could verify them without taking your word for it. An acceptable version names the Section (for example the hosting and architecture Section) and states the specific problem and the reason, with something checkable: which component is inadequate, what the plan assumes, why that fails, and what to change. For the costs, name the line and the figure you dispute and say what you would expect and why, in a way someone else could verify. Keep it proportionate to the size of the flaw and return it inside the window. If you think the whole premise is wrong, say so with reasons: it has to be argued, not asserted.",
  "A good answer: explains that the review does not point at a Section, give a reason or offer something verifiable (vague disagreement is not a correction); shows an acceptable version naming a Section, the specific problem, the reason and a checkable fix; mentions proportionality and the window; mentions arguing, not asserting, if disputing the premise; is constructive in tone. Penalise rewriting the Blueprint for them or inventing facts about the Blueprint.",
  "Review quality"));
items[3].questions.push(open(7,
  "A reviewer on the first paid rung, on the free Membership, completes one Expert Review and one Priority Review. Using the published figures, work out what reaches them in MOX and in pounds, how long before they can withdraw, and say what you would honestly tell them about income.",
  "An Expert Review is 490 MOX and a Priority Review 1,990 MOX. At the first paid rung the payout share is 0.55 and the free Membership takes 20%, leaving 80%. Expert Review: 490 × 0.55 × 0.80 = about 215 MOX. Priority Review: 1,990 × 0.55 × 0.80 = about 875 MOX. Together about 1,090 MOX. At the published 125 MOX to £1 that is about £8.70 (roughly £1.70 plus £7). The earnings are withdrawable after the hold, 14 days on the free Membership, and the conversion is at the rate published when they withdraw. Honestly, these are worked examples, not a promise of volume, and the amounts per review are small, so I would not promise an income; the rate can improve by climbing the Ladder (payout share rises to 0.95) and by a Membership that takes less and shortens the hold.",
  "A good answer: uses 490 and 1,990 MOX with 0.55 share and 80% after the 20% free Membership take; gets about 215 and about 875 MOX (total about 1,090); converts at 125 MOX to £1 to about £8.70 (about £1.70 and £7); states the 14-day hold on the free Membership; is honest that the page gives examples not a promise of volume and does not promise income; may mention that the rate depends on the published conversion and can change, and that climbing the Ladder or a Membership changes the share. Penalise arithmetic errors or any promise of earnings.",
  "Pay"));

const lessons = [
  [1, "The way in", ["Apply (about ten minutes), sit the Calibration (45 to 60 minutes), get accredited", "Everybody sits the Calibration; experience sets the rung and Band you start on", "Your first three reviews are read by a senior reviewer and paid in full", "No unpaid test: reviews are paid from the first one"]],
  [2, "The Ladder and the Queue", ["Five rungs: Candidate, Contributor, Reviewer, Senior, Principal", "You claim work from your Queue; leaving a job costs nothing", "Bands: C1 Simple, C2 Single system, C3 Complex, C4 Regulated", "A rung is earned, never bought"]],
  [3, "How a review works", ["A review is specific corrections, not a rewrite", "The fee is shown before you claim and released from Escrow on acceptance", "Disputes go to a senior reviewer", "Peer Review is free and earns MXP"]],
  [4, "Pay and conduct", ["MOX: 1 MOX = $0.01; 125 MOX to £1 at the published rate (can change)", "Share = job value × payout share (0.55 to 0.95) × what the Membership leaves", "Declare conflicts before accepting; concealment is what disqualifies", "A model may help you read or draft, but its output is not your review"]],
  [5, "Say only what is published", ["Quote the published five rungs; confirm anything beyond them", "Never promise income: the page gives worked examples, not a promise of volume", "Name a senior reviewer for disputes, not a rung", "Report any discrepancy so the pages can be corrected"]],
];

const prompts = new Set(), counts = {};
for (const it of items) for (const x of it.questions) {
  if (prompts.has(x.prompt)) throw new Error(`duplicate prompt: ${x.prompt}`);
  prompts.add(x.prompt);
  counts[x.level] = (counts[x.level] ?? 0) + 1;
}
const out = path.resolve("content/brand");
fs.mkdirSync(out, { recursive: true });
const file = { trackSlug: "moksy-reviewer-onboarding", status: "review", lessons: lessons.map(([level, title, syllabus]) => ({ level, title, syllabus })), items };
fs.writeFileSync(path.join(out, "moksy-reviewer-onboarding.json"), JSON.stringify(file, null, 1) + "\n");
console.log("moksy-reviewer-onboarding", prompts.size, "questions, per level:", JSON.stringify(counts));
